  "metadata": {
    "label": "Tobacco - T7 - Effective pharmacological intervention",
    "description": "Tobacco cessation pharmacotherapy intervention that provides cost-covered effective pharmacological interventions to all tobacco users who want to quit.\nThis scenario exposes the parameters for cessation medication policies that work by providing nicotine replacement therapy, prescription medications, and other pharmacological aids to support quit attempts.\nThe intervention reduces tobacco prevalence in the population by increasing successful quit rates through medication-assisted treatment, which in turn reduces the incidence rates of diabetes among susceptible individuals.\nPharmacological interventions include nicotine replacement therapy (patches, gum, lozenges), prescription medications (bupropion, varenicline), and other evidence-based cessation aids.\nThese medications are most effective when combined with behavioral support and when made accessible through healthcare coverage and reduced cost barriers.\nNote: For a comprehensive understanding of tobacco control impacts, this intervention should also be run for COPD, Asthma, and Cardiovascular Disease models.\n",
